5:[["$","$L10","4.1",{"topic":{"id":"4.1","title":"Introduction to Digital Evidence Forensic Toolkit (DEFT)","minutes":12,"summary":"Study DEFT as a forensic environment and a collection of tools—not as a magic button that makes evidence admissible.","sections":[{"title":"What DEFT is and is not","body":["DEFT, the Digital Evidence and Forensic Toolkit, is a Linux-based forensic distribution associated with a suite of examination and response utilities. Depending on the release, it includes or integrates tools for acquisition, filesystem examination, recovery, memory analysis, and network investigation. DEFT and DART are related historically but should not be treated as identical product names.","Many releases are old and unsupported. Their kernels, drivers, libraries, and tool versions may not handle current hardware, encryption, or application formats. This course explains transferable workflows rather than instructing learners to deploy an obsolete distribution on a real case. An archived environment can be useful for isolated training with supplied images."]},{"title":"The environment and its tools","body":["A forensic distribution packages a boot environment, utilities, dependencies, and menu launchers. The launcher does not establish that a tool is validated for the particular evidence source. Identify each tool’s actual version, input format, output behavior, and limitations. Filesystem examination, carving, memory analysis, and traffic analysis remain separate tasks requiring separate explanations.","DEFT-associated workflows may use tools such as The Sleuth Kit, Autopsy, carving utilities, and acquisition programs, but package contents vary by release. Record what is actually present rather than copying a menu list from another edition. The same caution applies to Kali: the distribution’s name is not a substitute for method validation."]},{"title":"A defensible classroom workflow","body":["Use a disposable virtual machine, a synthetic image, and a written exercise objective. Keep the original image read-only and create a separate output area. Record the environment, tool versions, input hash, settings, and output hashes. Do not connect personal accounts, real evidence, or an uncontrolled network to an unsupported environment.","For an exam, explain DEFT’s role, the major workflow stages, and the need for current validation. For a practical demonstration, the browser-based acquisition and integrity labs in this course model the reasoning without pretending to perform a physical disk acquisition. A simulated hash is labelled as such; real SHA-256 calculations use the browser’s cryptographic API."]}],"practice":{"title":"Inspect the toolkit inventory","scenario":"An archived DEFT VM contains several versions of a carving utility.","steps":["Record the distribution and tool versions actually installed.","Match one tool to acquisition, examination, or reporting.","Identify hardware and format limitations.","Create a read-only input and separate output plan for a synthetic image."],"debrief":"An inventory connects the tool to a task and version. A generic claim that “DEFT was used” does not make the examination reproducible."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","bsa"],"labId":"acquisition","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain what DEFT is and is not and apply it to an investigation.","Explain the environment and its tools and apply it to an investigation.","Explain a defensible classroom workflow and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.1","prompt":"What is the safest use of an old DEFT release today?","options":["Connect it directly to live production evidence.","Assume every included tool is current and validated.","Use an isolated training environment and verify each tool’s limitations.","Treat its name as proof of admissibility."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Archived distributions can teach concepts, but current casework requires supported methods and task-specific validation.","unit":4}}}]]
